{
  "dom_id": "step-q237514",
  "name": "Question 8",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/055cfb55d0e1d78762f7a51655031a982fdc2a9743f11331a239aff31e3b772c.png)\n\nIf the model above represents $4$ wholes, use the model to solve the following division problem. Express your answer as a mixed number in its simplest form.\n\n$4÷\\frac{3}{4}=$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "To calculate $4÷\\frac{3}{4}$, we first need to divide each of the $4$ wholes into $4$ equal parts.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/137a7924f2a2f463a014b3d6e289cc386b7981856febb02f5a1f7f7454e8ea1f.png)\n\n \n\nNext, we break this into groups of $3$:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14144806/assets/a569b682856cef52674f2fd517577c04a543a3ba385c6d904ec2955c9f792f53.png)\n\n \n\nNote the following: \n\n- We got $\\boxed{\\text{5}}$ full groups.\n\n- There is $1$ part left over out of a possible group of $3.$ So the fractional part of our answer is \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n4÷\\frac{3}{4}=\\boxed{\\text{5}}\\,\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
